          "choice": "DIAG",
          "sequence": 67,
          "body": "DIAG 65 Finding: the repeated failure is NOT in PropertyDocumenter — it is in test_autodoc_typed_inherited_instance_variables (tests/test_ext_autodoc.py, decorator at ~line 1837-1839; body starts `def test_autodoc_typed_inherited_instance_variables(app):` with options members/undoc-members/inherited-members and `do_autodoc(app, 'class', 'ta...`). That test documents a CLASS with inherited members, so it does not exercise the property code path you are changing. Two edits (v4 replace_file, v7 replace_file) plus two failures (seq 56 @v4, seq 67 @v7 — same nodeid, 1 failed/1 passed of 2 collected) therefore indicate the expected-output list in that test is stale or your edit changed class-level instance-variable emission, not that restify itself is wrong.\n\nRecommended next step for you as owner: read the saved failure detail (delivery_readiness final_test sequence 63 counts failed=1) to get the exact expected-vs-actual diff lines rather than re-running the same nodeid a third time unchanged. Two concrete hypotheses worth checking:\n1) If you changed InstanceAttributeDocumenter/AttributeDocumenter or something shared, the `:type:` lines now render with role markup (e.g. ':class:`~target.typehints.Point`') while this test still expects plain text — then THIS test's expectations must be updated in the same patch, and your property test should be added separately.\n2) If the diff shows no type-line change, the edit touched an unrelated code path and should be reverted to keep the patch minimal.\n\nMy review criteria remain: PropertyDocumenter.add_directive_header must use restify() for the `:type:` line, broken/None annotations must stay guarded (the existing TypeError/ValueError except block around inspect.signature must be preserved), a public test must assert the role markup form for a property return annotation, and no other autodoc tests may regress. I have made no edits to the tree."
        }
      ],
      "recipient_disposition": {
        "choice": "SKIP",
        "reason": "Diagnosis confirmed by the actual saved diff, and your hypothesis (1) is not the cause — the fix is unrelated to type-line markup. Real cause: I added two properties to the `Derived` fixture (typed_vars.py) so a property return annotation exists to test. `test_autodoc_typed_inherited_instance_variables` documents `Derived` with `inherited-members` and enumerates its members explicitly, so its expected list is now missing `Derived.prop1` / `Derived.prop2`. Error was `Left contains 14 more items` (actual has extra property blocks; expected lacks them). PropertyDocumenter change is correct and test_autodoc_typed_instance_variables already PASSES at v7 (1 passed / 1 failed of 2). Next: append the two property blocks to the inherited test's expectations, rerun both, then fix_patch for your independent review. No revert needed; restify is the right minimal change. Your review criteria (restify for the :type: line, preserved TypeError/ValueError guard, public test asserting role markup, no unrelated regressions) are all satisfied and I will make sure the regression set is green before publishing.",
